#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Refuse a commit that records files as DELETED while they are still sitting in the working tree.
#
# Why this exists. Many agents share this one working tree and one git index, so the standing advice
# is to build a commit in a private index (`GIT_INDEX_FILE=/tmp/idx git add … && git commit`) rather
# than let a concurrent `git add` ride along. That advice has a trap: a temp index path that does not
# exist yet starts EMPTY, not as a copy of HEAD. `git add` then puts your two files into an otherwise
# empty index, and `git commit` faithfully writes a tree containing exactly those two files — which
# means every other tracked file in the repo is committed as a deletion. The working tree is never
# touched, so nothing looks wrong until someone checks out that commit.
#
# The fix for the person writing the commit is `git read-tree HEAD` right after setting the temp path
# (or, better, just `git commit -- <paths>`, which needs no temp index at all). The fix for everyone
# else is this hook, because that advice has been skipped at least twice.
#
# The predicate is deliberately narrow. A real deletion — `git rm`, deleting a directory, a refactor
# that removes files — takes the file off disk too, so it will NOT trip this. Only a staged deletion
# whose path still EXISTS in the working tree is reported, and that combination is almost always the
# empty-index bug. The rare legitimate case is `git rm --cached` (untrack but keep the file), which is
# what --no-verify is for.

if ! git rev-parse --verify HEAD >/dev/null 2>&1; then
	exit 0 # initial commit: nothing to compare against
fi

# A merge's deletions are computed by git from the two sides, not by a hand-built index, so the bug
# this guards against cannot arise here — while a conflicted delete/modify CAN legitimately leave a
# staged deletion beside a file still on disk. FRIZZ.md mandates merging branches back into main, so
# exempt merges rather than have the documented workflow trip a guard aimed at something else.
if [ -e "$(git rev-parse --git-dir)/MERGE_HEAD" ]; then
	exit 0
fi

# Staged deletions whose file is nonetheless still present on disk.
phantom=$(git diff --cached --diff-filter=D --name-only -z HEAD |
	while IFS= read -r -d '' path; do
		[ -e "$path" ] && printf '%s\n' "$path"
	done)

count=$(printf '%s' "$phantom" | grep -c . || true)
[ "$count" -eq 0 ] && exit 0

tracked=$(git ls-tree -r --name-only HEAD | wc -l | tr -d ' ')

cat >&2 <<EOF

╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
║  COMMIT BLOCKED — it would delete $count file(s) that still exist on disk
╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝

This commit records $count of the repo's $tracked tracked files as DELETED, but every
one of them is still present in the working tree. That is almost never a real
deletion — it is the signature of committing through an EMPTY index.

Most likely cause: a private index that was never seeded from HEAD, e.g.
    GIT_INDEX_FILE=/tmp/idx git add <paths> && git commit     # <-- /tmp/idx starts EMPTY

Fix it with whichever fits:

  1. Just use a path-scoped commit — no temp index needed, and a concurrent
     \`git add\` by another agent still cannot ride along:
         git commit -m "…" -- <paths>

  2. If you genuinely need a private index (e.g. \`git apply --cached\` to slice
     one hunk out of a file another agent is also editing), seed it first:
         export GIT_INDEX_FILE=/tmp/idx; rm -f "\$GIT_INDEX_FILE"
         git read-tree HEAD          # <-- the step that was missing
         git add <paths>
         git diff --cached HEAD --stat   # verify EXACTLY your files
         git commit -F msg

First few paths this commit would delete:
$(printf '%s\n' "$phantom" | head -8 | sed 's/^/    /')

If you really did mean to untrack these while keeping them on disk
(\`git rm --cached\`), re-run with --no-verify.

EOF
exit 1
